# The Moral Weight of Privacy

Privacy is often mistaken for concealment —  
a shadow where wrongdoing hides.  
But in truth, it’s the space where dignity lives.  
To exist freely, one must first exist unseen,  
long enough to become authentic.

Zero-knowledge restores that space.  
It allows integrity without exposure,  
honesty without confession.  
It doesn’t protect what’s false —  
it protects what’s human.

The moral challenge of our age isn’t how to share,  
but how to withhold with care.  
We’ve learned to make transparency a virtue,
